Generics let you write reusable code that works with any type while keeping type safety.

```typescript
// Without generics: lose type info
function first(arr: any[]): any {
    return arr[0];  // Returns 'any' — no type safety!
}

// With generics: preserves type
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined {
    return arr[0];
}

const num = first([1, 2, 3]);        // Type: number
const str = first(['a', 'b', 'c']);  // Type: string

// Generic with constraint
function getLength<T extends { length: number }>(item: T): number {
    return item.length;
}
getLength('hello');     // OK: string has .length
getLength([1, 2, 3]);  // OK: array has .length
getLength(42);          // Error: number has no .length

// Generic interface
interface Repository<T> {
    findById(id: string): Promise<T | null>;
    findAll(): Promise<T[]>;
    create(item: Omit<T, 'id'>): Promise<T>;
}

class UserRepo implements Repository<User> {
    async findById(id: string) { /* ... */ }
    async findAll() { /* ... */ }
    async create(data: Omit<User, 'id'>) { /* ... */ }
}
```

Generics are used everywhere: `Array<T>`, `Promise<T>`, `Map<K,V>`, React's `useState<T>`. They're the key to writing reusable, type-safe libraries.

Discriminated unions are the most powerful TypeScript pattern. Each variant has a literal `type` field that TypeScript uses to narrow types automatically.

```typescript
type Result<T> =
    | { status: 'loading' }
    | { status: 'success'; data: T }
    | { status: 'error'; message: string };

function renderResult(result: Result<User[]>) {
    switch (result.status) {
        case 'loading':
            return <Spinner />;
        case 'success':
            return result.data.map(u => <div>{u.name}</div>);
            // TypeScript knows result.data exists here!
        case 'error':
            return <p>Error: {result.message}</p>;
            // TypeScript knows result.message exists here!
    }
}
```

**Why powerful:**
- TypeScript narrows the type in each branch automatically
- Exhaustiveness checking: if you add a new status and forget to handle it, TypeScript warns you
- No more `if (data && !error && !loading)` chains
- Self-documenting: each state is explicit

```typescript
// API events
type WebSocketEvent =
    | { type: 'connected'; sessionId: string }
    | { type: 'message'; data: string; from: string }
    | { type: 'error'; code: number; reason: string }
    | { type: 'disconnected' };
```

Use discriminated unions for: API states, events, actions (Redux), AST nodes, form states — anything with multiple variants.

Built-in types that transform other types.

```typescript
interface User {
    id: number;
    name: string;
    email: string;
    role: 'admin' | 'user';
}

// Partial<T> — all properties optional
type UpdateUser = Partial<User>;
// { id?: number; name?: string; email?: string; role?: ... }

// Pick<T, K> — select specific properties
type UserPreview = Pick<User, 'id' | 'name'>;
// { id: number; name: string }

// Omit<T, K> — remove properties
type CreateUser = Omit<User, 'id'>;
// { name: string; email: string; role: ... }

// Required<T> — all properties required
// Readonly<T> — all properties readonly

// Record<K, V> — object type with keys K and values V
type Permissions = Record<string, boolean>;
// { [key: string]: boolean }

// ReturnType<T> — extract function return type
function getConfig() { return { port: 3000, host: 'localhost' }; }
type Config = ReturnType<typeof getConfig>;
// { port: number; host: string }

// Exclude / Extract — filter union types
type NonNull = Exclude<string | number | null, null>;
// string | number
```

Most used in practice: `Partial` (for update operations), `Omit` (for create operations without ID), `Pick` (for API responses with fewer fields), `Record` (for dictionaries).

Zod lets you define a schema ONCE and get both runtime validation AND TypeScript types from it.

```typescript
import { z } from 'zod';

// Define schema
const UserSchema = z.object({
    name: z.string().min(1).max(100),
    email: z.string().email(),
    age: z.number().int().min(0).optional(),
    role: z.enum(['admin', 'user', 'moderator']),
});

// Extract TypeScript type FROM schema (single source of truth!)
type User = z.infer<typeof UserSchema>;
// { name: string; email: string; age?: number; role: 'admin'|'user'|'moderator' }

// Runtime validation
function createUser(input: unknown): User {
    return UserSchema.parse(input);  // Throws if invalid
}

// Safe validation (no throw)
const result = UserSchema.safeParse(data);
if (result.success) {
    console.log(result.data.name);  // Fully typed!
} else {
    console.log(result.error.issues);  // Validation errors
}

// Compose schemas
const CreateUserSchema = UserSchema.omit({ id: true });
const UpdateUserSchema = UserSchema.partial();
```

Zod eliminates the duplication between TypeScript types and validation logic. Define once, get type safety at compile time AND runtime. Used by tRPC, React Hook Form, and most modern TypeScript projects.

Both define object shapes, but they have different strengths.

**Interface — best for objects and classes:**
```typescript
interface User {
    id: number;
    name: string;
}

// Extend
interface Admin extends User {
    permissions: string[];
}

// Declaration merging (interface can be extended later)
interface User {
    email: string;  // Added to existing User interface!
}
```

**Type — best for unions, tuples, complex types:**
```typescript
// Union (interface CAN'T do this)
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive' | 'banned';
type StringOrNumber = string | number;

// Tuple
type Point = [number, number];

// Mapped/conditional types
type Nullable<T> = { [K in keyof T]: T[K] | null };

// Intersection
type AdminUser = User & { permissions: string[] };
```

**Rules of thumb:**
- Object shapes → `interface` (better error messages, extends, declaration merging)
- Unions, tuples, primitives → `type` (interface can't do these)
- Library APIs → `interface` (consumers can extend via declaration merging)
- Complex type operations → `type` (mapped types, conditional types)

In practice, either works for most cases. Pick one convention and be consistent. Many teams use `type` for everything — it's simpler.

```typescript
// Basic props
interface ButtonProps {
    label: string;
    onClick: () => void;
    variant?: 'primary' | 'secondary';  // Optional with union
    disabled?: boolean;
}

function Button({ label, onClick, variant = 'primary', disabled }: ButtonProps) {
    return <button onClick={onClick} disabled={disabled}>{label}</button>;
}

// Children prop
interface CardProps {
    title: string;
    children: React.ReactNode;  // Accepts any renderable content
}

// Extending HTML element props
interface InputProps extends React.InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement> {
    label: string;
    error?: string;
}

function Input({ label, error, ...rest }: InputProps) {
    return (
        <div>
            <label>{label}</label>
            <input {...rest} />
            {error && <span>{error}</span>}
        </div>
    );
}
// Now accepts ALL native input props + label + error

// Event handlers
const handleChange = (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => {
    console.log(e.target.value);
};

const handleSubmit = (e: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) => {
    e.preventDefault();
};
```

Tip: hover over props in VS Code to see their types. Use `React.ComponentProps<typeof Button>` to extract props from an existing component.
